# Gitleaks fingerprint allowlist.
#
# This is the reviewable escape hatch. Each line silences ONE specific finding,
# identified by a fingerprint that gitleaks prints when it blocks you:
#
#   <commit-sha>:<file-path>:<rule-id>:<line-number>
#
# For a finding in the working tree (pre-commit, not yet committed) the
# fingerprint has no commit component:
#
#   <file-path>:<rule-id>:<line-number>
#
# Copy the fingerprint exactly as printed, add it below, and — this is the part
# that makes the hatch safe — write a comment above it saying WHY it is not a
# secret. Because this file is committed, every exception goes through code
# review. That is the difference between an escape hatch and a hole.
#
# Do NOT add a fingerprint to silence a real credential. If it is real, the fix
# is to remove it, move it to an env var, and rotate it. A fingerprint here just
# hides it from the scanner while it stays readable to the whole internet.
#
# Prefer an inline `gitleaks:allow` comment when you can edit the offending line
# — it keeps the justification next to the code. Use this file when you cannot
# (generated files, fixtures, binary-ish content, historical commits).
#
